hypotheses.statement:
Let $D\ge2$ and $1\le k<D$ be integers. If $U$ is uniform on $S^{D-1}$ and $P$ is any $k$-by-$D$ matrix satisfying $PP^{\mathsf T}=I_k$, then $PU$ has Lebesgue density $$\label{p:coordinate-formula}
 c(D,k)(1-\left\lVert u\right\rVert^2)^{(D-k-2)/2}\mathbf 1_{\{\left\lVert u\right\rVert<1\}},
 \qquad
 c(D,k)=\frac{\Gamma(D/2)}
 {\pi^{k/2}\Gamma((D-k)/2)}.$$ The direction of the component of $U$ in $\ker P$ is uniform on its unit sphere and is independent of the pair consisting of $PU$ and the length of that complementary component. In particular, its conditional law given $PU$ is uniform.

Put $n=d-1$ for $d\ge2$. There is an absolute $a>0$ such that, for every $c\in S^{d-1}$ and $0<r\le2$, $$\label{p:cap-mass-two-sided}
 (ar)^n\le \sigma\bigl(B(c,r)\bigr)\le\min\{1,r^n\}.$$ For every ambient center $z\in\mathbb R^d$ and radius $r>0$, $$\label{p:ambient-cap-mass}
 \sigma\bigl(B(z,r)\bigr)\le\min\{1,(2r)^n\}.$$ An output direction independent of the signal has probability at most $\epsilon^n$ of angular error at most $\epsilon$, averaged over $\sigma$.

Let $k,m\ge1$ and $k+m+1\le d$. Fix a $d$-by-$m$ matrix $E$ with orthonormal columns, and draw $P\sim\vartheta_{d,k}$. The $k$-by-$m$ matrix $PE$ has Lebesgue density $$\label{p:frame-density-exact}
 \left(\prod_{j=0}^{m-1}
 \frac{\Gamma((d-j)/2)}
 {\pi^{k/2}\Gamma((d-j-k)/2)}\right)
 \det(I_k-ZZ^{\mathsf T})^{(d-k-m-1)/2}
 \mathbf 1_{\{I_k-ZZ^{\mathsf T}>0\}}.$$ Here $>0$ means positive definite. In particular, this density is bounded by $(C\sqrt d)^{km}$.*


*If $x_1,\ldots,x_{m+1}\in\mathbb R^d$ are affinely independent and $$\delta_i=\operatorname{dist}\bigl(x_i,\operatorname{aff}(x_1,\ldots,x_{i-1})\bigr),
 \qquad 2\le i\le m+1,$$ then the joint Lebesgue density of the columns $P(x_i-x_1)$, $2\le i\le m+1$, is bounded by $$\label{p:frame-difference-density}
 (C\sqrt d)^{km}\prod_{i=2}^{m+1}\delta_i^{-k}.$$

Let $d,k,q$ be integers and $\beta$ a real number with $k\ge1$, $q\ge2$, and $$\label{p:frame-margins}
 k+q\le d,\qquad 0<\beta\le d,\qquad
 \beta-k-(q-2)\ge1.$$ Let $\nu$ be a finite positive Borel measure supported in $B(z,R)$, $R>0$, satisfying <a href="#p:all-ball-mass" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="p:all-ball-mass">[p:all-ball-mass]</a> for some $K\ge0$. Assume that $P_\#\nu$ is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure for $\vartheta_{d,k}$-almost every $P$. There is a jointly measurable version $g(P,u)$ of its density such that $$\label{p:frame-moment-bound}
 \left(\int_{\mathcal V_{d,k}}\int_{\mathbb R^k}g(P,u)^q\,du\,
 d\vartheta_{d,k}(P)\right)^{1/q}
 \le C^d(C\sqrt d)^{k(1-1/q)}
 K R^{\beta-k(1-1/q)}.$$ For every $P$, this version vanishes when $\left\lVert u-Pz\right\rVert>R$.

For sufficiently large $d$, put $$n=d-1,\qquad k=q=\lfloor d/4\rfloor,\qquad \beta=n-q.$$ Let $0\le f\le1$ be Borel on $S^{d-1}$, and suppose for $K\ge0$ that $$\int_{B(b,t)}f\,d\sigma\le Kt^\beta
 \qquad(b\in\mathbb R^d,\ t>0).$$ For $z\in\mathbb R^d$, $R>0$, and $E=B(z,R)$, the projection of $\mathbf 1_E f\sigma$ by $P\sim\vartheta_{d,k}$ has a jointly measurable Lebesgue density $g(P,u)$ satisfying $$\label{p:frame-n30-bound}
 \left\lVert g\right\rVert_{L^q(\vartheta_{d,k}\,du)}
 \le C^d(C\sqrt d)^{k(1-1/q)}
 K R^{\beta-k(1-1/q)}.$$

Let $\mathcal V$ and $\mathcal W$ be finite state sets with $\left\lvert \mathcal W\right\rvert\le2^M$. For each $v\in\mathcal V$, let $K_{v,w}$ be block kernels of the type in <a href="#p:cap-reference-probabilities" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="p:cap-reference-probabilities">[p:cap-reference-probabilities]</a>, with likelihoods $f_{v,w}$. Let $\eta_v$ be finite positive measures on the sphere and suppose $$\eta_v\le\sum_i a_{v,i}\mu_{c_{v,i},r_{v,i}},\qquad
 \mathcal P=\sum_{v,i}a_{v,i}(2/r_{v,i})^\alpha<\infty,$$ where each sum is countable, $a_{v,i}\ge0$, and $0<r_{v,i}\le2$. Define the outgoing measures by $$\eta'_w(E)=\sum_v\int_E f_{v,w}(s)\,\eta_v(ds).$$ Then the $\eta'_w$ admit countable positive cap dominations whose total cost $\mathcal P'$ satisfies $$\label{p:cap-cost-multiplier}
 \mathcal P'\le\mathcal P\exp(Cd)2^{M/p}.$$

For all sufficiently large $d$, let a learner obey the observation and information restrictions in Section <a href="#p:geometry-model" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="p:geometry-model">2</a>, with $0<\epsilon\le1/10$, any nonnegative integer $M$ bits of state, and horizon $T$, and let $\mathsf T$ be its stopping index. For every fixed integer $0\le t\le T$, $$\label{p:cap-fixed-stop-explicit}
 \mathbb P\{\mathsf T=t\text{ and }\arccos\langle \widehat S,S\rangle\le\epsilon\}
 \le\exp(Cd)(\epsilon/2)^\alpha
       \left[\exp(Cd)2^{M/p}\right]^{\lceil t/k\rceil}.$$ In particular, for a sequence $M(d)=o(d^2)$ the right side is eventually at most $$\label{p:cap-fixed-stop-asymptotic}
 \exp\bigl(Cd(1+\lceil t/k\rceil)\bigr)(\epsilon/2)^\alpha,$$ with an absolute $C$ and a dimension threshold that may depend on the memory sequence.

For every $z\in\mathbb R^d$ and $0<r\le1/10$, $$\label{p:riesz-cap}
 \sigma(B_r(z))\le C^d r^{d-1}.$$ Every measurable $h:S^{d-1}\to[0,1]$ has finite potential. Moreover, for $0<\epsilon\le1/10$, $$\label{p:riesz-inner}
 \sup_{z\in\mathbb R^d}
 \int_{\left\lVert s-z\right\rVert\le\epsilon}\left\lVert s-z\right\rVert^{-\alpha}\,d\sigma(s)
 \le C^d\epsilon^{d-1-\alpha}.$$

Let $h:S^{d-1}\to[0,1]$ be measurable, $z\in\mathbb R^d$, $\rho>0$, and let $F$ be an affine subspace through $z$ of dimension $\ell\le q-1$. Then $F\cap B_\rho(z)$ has zero $h\,d\sigma$ mass, and $$\label{p:riesz-distance-bound}
 \int_{B_\rho(z)}
       \operatorname{dist}(s,F)^{-k}h(s)\,d\sigma(s)
 \le C^d V(h)\rho^{\alpha-k}.$$

Let $z\in\mathbb R^d$, $\rho>0$, let $H\subset S^{d-1}\cap B_\rho(z)$ be measurable, and let $h:S^{d-1}\to[0,1]$ be measurable. Fix these objects before drawing a $k$ by $d$ standard Gaussian matrix $X$. The pushforward of $h\mathbf 1_H\,d\sigma$ by $s\mapsto Xs$ admits a single jointly Borel function $$R:\mathbb R^{k\times d}\times\mathbb R^k\longrightarrow[0,\infty]$$ such that $R(X,\cdot)$ is its Lebesgue density for every full-row-rank $X$, and, for every fixed $b\in\mathbb R^k$, $$\label{p:riesz-density-bound}
 \left(\mathbb E_X R(X,Xz+b)^q\right)^{1/q}
 \le C^d V(h)\rho^{\alpha-k}.$$ The same function $R$ is used for all $b$. Moreover, $$\label{p:riesz-density-support}
 R(X,y)=0
 \quad\hbox{if}\quad
 \left\lVert y-Xz\right\rVert>\left\lVert X\right\rVert_{\mathrm{op}}\rho .$$

For $1\le i\le N$, $v\in\mathcal V_{i-1}$, $z\in\mathbb R^d$, and $\rho>0$, let $H_\rho(z)=\{s\in S^{d-1}:\rho/2<\left\lVert s-z\right\rVert\le\rho\}$. Then $$\label{p:riesz-shell-bound}
 \int_{H_\rho(z)}
   \frac{h_{i-1}(v,s)}{\left\lVert s-z\right\rVert^{\alpha}}\,d\sigma(s)
 \le C^d W^{1/q}V_i.$$

